alth-poverty-divide-american-cities.html', 'snippet': 'The biggest metropolitan areas are now the most unequal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1980, highly paid workers in Binghamton, N.Y., earned about four and a half times what low-wage workers there did. The gap between them, in a region full of I.B.M. executives and manufacturing jobs, was about the same as the gap between the workers near the top and the bottom in metro New York.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Watch 4 Decades of Inequality Drive American Cities Apart', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Big Cities Have Prospered, But Inequality Has Soared', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Binghamton (NY)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Fairfield (Conn)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Houston (Tex)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Francisco (Calif)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Jose (Calif)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (DC)', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Urban Areas', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-12-02T10:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Upshot', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline':