person': [{'firstname': 'Marc', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6bd59f89-bfee-54ee-9dff-d1aca6506cd7', 'word_count': 2953, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6bd59f89-bfee-54ee-9dff-d1aca6506cd7'}, {'abstract': 'In 1989, a BLU-97 cluster-munition bomblet exploded in a Kansas factory, killing two workers. Their fates were soon shared by American service members in the Persian Gulf War.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/04/magazine/cluster-bomb-kansas.html', 'snippet': 'In 1989, a BLU-97 cluster-munition bomblet exploded in a Kansas factory, killing two workers. Their fates were soon shared by American service members in the Persian Gulf War.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Inside the government-owned Kansas Army Ammunition Plant, hundreds of civilians worked in close quarters on the assembly lines. The plant, spread among a series of huge block buildings on a secure 14,000-acre site in Parsons, Kan., was one of the centers of production for America’s cluster munitions. It was 1989, and the Pentagon was still keeping arsenals filled for a potential war against the Soviet Union. Factories in other parts of the country made parts and pieces of the weapons, but in Parsons they all came together, a lethal product assuming final form.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/04/magazine/04atwar-kansasfactory-05/04atwar-kansasfactory-05-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/04/magazine/04atwar-kansasfactory-05/04atwar-kansasfactory-05-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/04/magazine/04atwar-kansasfactory-05/04atwar-kansasfactory-05-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/04/magazine/04atwar-kansasfactory-05/04atwar-kansasfactory-05-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1368, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/04/magazine/04atwar-kansasfactory-05/04atwar-kansasfactory-05-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/12/04/magazine/04atwar-kansasfactory-05/04atwar-kansasfactory-05-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/04/magazine/04atwar-kansasfactory-05/04atwar-kansasfactory-05-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'These Cluster Bombs Started Killing Civilians Before They Left U.S. Soil', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Workplace Hazards and Violations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cluster Munitions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Factories and Manufacturing', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Defense Department', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kansas', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-12-04T10:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Ismay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ismay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/50108a3f-e595-5a14-9c09-5457e808ffe7', 'word_count': 1343, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50108a3f-e595-5a14-9c09-5457e808ffe7'}, {'abstract': 'The weapons are notorious for their effects on civilians. But five years of reporting and hundreds of interviews have revealed they’ve also killed and wounded scores of Americans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/04/magazine/cluster-munitions-history.html', 'snippet': 'The weapons are notorious for their effects on civilians. But five years of reporting and hundreds of interviews have revealed they’ve also killed and wounded scores of Americans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Staff Sgt. Michael S. Crick huddled in the howling wind and wrote in his diary. It was just past noon on Feb. 26, 1991, the third day of the American-led invasion of Iraq during the Persian Gulf war. The day before, a French and American force had seized As Salman airfield, an Iraqi military installation about 70 miles from the Saudi Arabian border.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/03/magazine/03atwar-clustermunitions-image-06/03atwar-clustermunitions-image-06-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 424, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/03/magazine/03atwar-clustermunitions-image-06/03atwar-clustermunitions-image-06-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 424}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/03/magazine/03atwar-clustermunitions-image-06/03atwar-clustermunitions-image-06-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 723, 'width': 1024, 'subType': '