': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/37679a45-be17-5afa-9c98-65b83f9e1c0c', 'word_count': 455, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37679a45-be17-5afa-9c98-65b83f9e1c0c'}, {'abstract': 'David Fincher’s eerie drama is on FXM. And the third part of a Christmastime trilogy premieres on Netflix.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/05/arts/television/whats-on-tv-thursday-gone-girl-and-a-new-holiday-favorite.html', 'snippet': 'David Fincher’s eerie drama is on FXM. And the third part of a Christmastime trilogy premieres on Netflix.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GONE GIRL (2014) 7:40 p.m. on FXM. Ben Affleck stars in this drama as Nick Dunne, a small-town Midwestern man who comes home one day to find his wife, Amy (Rosamund Pike), missing. Nick becomes a suspect in her disappearance, with the only evidence as to Amy’s whereabouts coming from her diary entries. These detail the deterioration of her marriage and her fear of her husband. And while Nick maintains his innocence, it becomes clear that Amy may not be a reliable narrator. Based on the 2012 novel by Gillian Flynn, who also wrote the screenplay, “Gone Girl” is “a ghastly vision,” Manohla Dargis wrote in her New York Times review. Dargis added that the director, David Fincher, “fashions an ever more haunted, haunting world that wavers so violently between ordinariness and aberration that, as in his other movies, the two soon blur.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/05/arts/05gonegirl/merlin_88030892_1c3cd8bd-bb18-490f-877d-77b6707cbb9b-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 393, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/05/arts/05gonegirl/merlin_88030892_1c3cd8bd-bb18-490f-877d-77b6707cbb9b-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheigh