': 'article', 'news_desk': 'NYTNow', 'section_name': 'Briefing', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Ives', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ives', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/9dafe94f-5389-5968-b54d-c0aaf0ff769d', 'word_count': 1578, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9dafe94f-5389-5968-b54d-c0aaf0ff769d'}, {'abstract': '“The Marvelous Mrs. Maisel” is back for a third season. And a long-lost adaptation of “Wuthering” starring Richard Burton has been recovered.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/06/arts/television/whats-on-tv-friday-mrs-maisel-and-wuthering-heights.html', 'snippet': '“The Marvelous Mrs. Maisel” is back for a third season. And a long-lost adaptation of “Wuthering” starring Richard Burton has been recovered.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE MARVELOUS MRS. MAISEL Stream on Amazon. Mrs. Midge Maisel is back for another season, but this time … she’s on tour. Accompanied by her sidekick/manager Susie (Alex Borstein), Midge — played by Rachel Brosnahan — accompanies a musician on his tour across the U.S. As she enjoys glamorous cities and navigates the challenges of life on the road, Midge’s husband and parents are adjusting to the courses of their own lives. But one thing remains abundantly clear: Mrs. Maisel has no intention of slowing down. “This show is nothing but momentum,” James Poniewozik wrote of the previous season in his New York Times review. “It jabbers, it twirls, its heels click across the room,” he added. “It would love to stay and chat, but it’s gotta get somewhere.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/06/arts/06TVmrsmaisel/06mrsmaisel-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge