ado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/11c0f2f4-47c7-511e-901f-836a48eee5b7', 'word_count': 196, 'uri': 'nyt://article/11c0f2f4-47c7-511e-901f-836a48eee5b7'}, {'abstract': 'Chinese officials have released social media videos, blistering editorials and attacks on researchers in a push to counter evidence of its Muslim internment drive.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/09/world/asia/china-camps-muslims.html', 'snippet': 'Chinese officials have released social media videos, blistering editorials and attacks on researchers in a push to counter evidence of its Muslim internment drive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — On Twitter and YouTube, with slick videos and strident editorials, the Chinese government has gone on the offensive to reject mounting evidence that it is detaining Muslims in droves, depicting its critics as players in a Western conspiracy. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/09/world/09china-xinjiang-1/merlin_165659412_9b619f14-8c22-40f1-acde-b296eab596a3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/09/world/09china-xinjiang-1/merlin_165659412_9b619f14-8c22-40f1-acde-b296eab596a3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/09/world/09china-xinjiang-1/merlin_165659412_9b619f14-8c22-40f1-acde-b296eab596a3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, '