h': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/14/slideshows/11readers-YIP/15YIP-October-slide-KNHS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/14/slideshows/11readers-YIP/15YIP-October-slide-KNHS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/14/slideshows/11readers-YIP/15YIP-October-slide-KNHS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/14/slideshows/11readers-YIP/15YIP-October-slide-KNHS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/12/14/slideshows/11readers-YIP/15YIP-October-slide-KNHS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/14/slideshows/11readers-YIP/15YIP-October-slide-KNHS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'From 500,000 Photos to 116: How Our Editors Distill the Year in Pictures', 'kicker': 'Times Insider', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editing 500,000 Photos Down to 116', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hong Kong Protests (2019)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Looram, Meaghan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scales, Jeffrey Henson', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mills, Doug', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-12-11T10:00:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'NewsDesk', 'section_name': 'Reader Center', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lara Takenaga', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Takenaga', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a485627-3e7e-5c75-9370-57badec968e7', 'word_count': 951, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a485627-3e7e-5c75-9370-57badec968e7'}, {'abstract': 'Known for its intelligence-gathering operations during World War II, the O.S.S. also helped shape the look of modern life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/11/t-magazine/us-government-20th-century-design.html', 'snippet': 'Known for its intelligence-gathering operations during World War II, the O.S.S. also helped shape the look of modern life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE OFFICE OF STRATEGIC SERVICES was the first centralized foreign intelligence agency in the United States, the direct precursor to the Central Intelligence Agency. Founded during World War II by order of President Franklin D. Roosevelt, the O.S.S. (or the Coordinator of Information, as it was originally called) was established “to collect, analyze, and correlate information and data bearing on national security, to make such data available to the President and such other officials as determined by the President, and to perform related supplementary activities.” And yet its role soon became much larger and more influential. The head of the O.S.S., Colonel William “Wild Bill” Donovan, quickly realized that the information produced by the global war, fought on multiple fronts and territories, needed to be painstakingly abstracted, graphically depicted and efficiently presented. The O.S.S. mission expanded to include not only information gathering through research and counterintelligence but also packaging and interpreting that information for the president and other key decision makers. Propaganda played a crucial role during World War II, with the O.S.S. leading efforts to demoralize the enemy and encourage resistance in Axis-controlled countries. They created a stamp bearing the face of Hitler rendered as a skull, produced radio broadcasts in German, spread pamphlets announcing a German general’s resignation and made films for new military recruits, such as the Hollywood producer Darryl F. Zanuck’s circa 1943 “Organization of the Army,” which was commissioned by General George C. Marshall. Through its manipulation of the art of information, the agency helped shape the look and philosophy of American imperialism. In doing so, the O.S.S. assembled a concentration of acumen and talent that rivaled those of the iconic schools and institutes that propagated design in the 20th century. It was the Bauhaus, but for war.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/09/t-magazine/09tmag-oss-slide-Z7EY/09tmag-oss-slide-Z7EY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 453, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/09/t-magazine/09tmag-oss-slide-Z7EY/09tmag-oss-slide-Z7EY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 453}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/09/t-magazine/09tmag-oss-slide-Z7EY/09tmag-oss-slide-Z7EY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 772, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/09/t-magazine/09tmag-oss-slide-Z7EY/09tmag-oss-slide-Z7EY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1545, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/09/t-magazine/09tmag-oss-slide-Z7EY/09tmag-oss-slide-Z7EY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/12/09/t-magazine/09tmag-oss-slide-Z7EY/09tmag-oss-slide-Z7EY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/09/t-magazine/09tmag-oss-slide-Z7EY/09tmag-oss-slide-Z7EY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Curious Case of the U.S. Government’s Influence on 20th-Century Design', 'kicker': 'Histories and Happenings', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Design', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Espionage and Intelligence Services', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Central Intelligence Agency', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Child, Julia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Child, Paul', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Disney, Walt', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Saarinen, Eero', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roosevelt, Franklin Delano', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-12-11T10:00:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nikil Saval', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nikil', 'middlena