: None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e8544ae-dbd1-5d97-913b-4f12f82e4b4d', 'word_count': 946, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e8544ae-dbd1-5d97-913b-4f12f82e4b4d'}, {'abstract': 'The paintings on an Indonesian island are at least 43,900 years old and depict humanoid figures with animal-like features in a hunting scene.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/11/science/cave-art-indonesia.html', 'snippet': 'The paintings on an Indonesian island are at least 43,900 years old and depict humanoid figures with animal-like features in a hunting scene.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In December 2017, Hamrullah, an archaeologist on an Indonesian government survey, was exploring a cave system in Sulawesi, a large island in central Indonesia. He noticed a tantalizing opening in the ceiling above him. A skilled spelunker, Hamrullah (who only uses one name, like many Indonesians) climbed through the gap into an uncharted chamber. There, he laid eyes on a painting that is upending our understanding of prehistoric humans.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/11/science/11CAVEART03/merlin_165794409_560df4f9-2558-4051-aea3-15c404cb36df-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 788, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/11/science/11CAVEART03/merlin_165794409_560df4f9-2558-4051-aea3-15c404cb36df-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 788}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/11/science/11CAVEART03/merlin_165794409_560df4f9-2558-4051-aea3-15c404cb36df-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 780, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/11/science/11CAVEART03/me