Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-12-11T21:08:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Obits', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katharine Q. Seelye', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katharine', 'middlename': 'Q.', 'lastname': 'Seelye', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/02b3a5c6-98b8-5258-8eec-b46c767acd03', 'word_count': 1103, 'uri': 'nyt://article/02b3a5c6-98b8-5258-8eec-b46c767acd03'}, {'abstract': 'Everywhere you look, there are cleaner, more private and more inclusive loos. Thank social justice warriors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/11/opinion/public-restrooms.html', 'snippet': 'Everywhere you look, there are cleaner, more private and more inclusive loos. Thank social justice warriors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the past few years, something amazing has been quietly happening across the land. Public restrooms in America — long a flash point of exclusion by race, gender, class and ability — have gotten much, much better. Thanks to an increasingly effective campaign by a range of activists, America’s public bathrooms are now cleaner, more plentiful, more private, and more accessible and inclusive than ever. In many places, they’re even great.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/11/opinion/11manjooWeb/11manjooWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/11/opinion/11manjooWeb/11manjooWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/11/opinion/11manjooWeb/11manjooWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jum