'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-12-12T19:08:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9857df29-94ef-5f55-9b2f-65f23510b85f', 'word_count': 894, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9857df29-94ef-5f55-9b2f-65f23510b85f'}, {'abstract': 'A collector brings together the star-struck and the heartbroken in a constantly evolving exhibition.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/12/arts/design/show-us-your-wall.html', 'snippet': 'A collector brings together the star-struck and the heartbroken in a constantly evolving exhibition.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I hope you don’t have a seizure,” Candace Carmel Barasch playfully warned a visitor entering her Park Avenue apartment, where many of the vibrantly hued artworks blink and buzz on the walls. Alex Da Corte’s theatrical tableau of a candle in a window is outlined in neon tubing so intensely colored it almost hurts the eyes. Cory Arcangel’s portrait of Miley Cyrus on a flat-screen TV uses obsolete technology to create a rippling lake effect that could induce wistfulness — and a touch of seasickness.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/13/arts/13showwall-barasch1/13showwall-barasch1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/13/arts/13showwall-barasch1/13showwall-barasch1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/13/arts/13showwall-barasch1/13showw