': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mihir Zaveri', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mihir', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zaveri', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8defac22-abc1-5c92-8c21-10403eed3508', 'word_count': 578, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8defac22-abc1-5c92-8c21-10403eed3508'}, {'abstract': 'We can’t excuse an abuse of power just because it supports our side.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/16/opinion/first-amendment-impeachment.html', 'snippet': 'We can’t excuse an abuse of power just because it supports our side.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week, the House Judiciary Committee debated articles of impeachment drawn from a densely argued, sharply worded 300-page report that the Democratic majority on the House Intelligence Committee produced. It is an investigative tour de force, written for posterity. In considerable detail, using interviews and records, it describes, for example, how President Trump’s top personal adviser, Rudolph W. Giuliani, traipsed across Europe, circumventing diplomats as he furthered the president’s direct political interests. It also delves into the circumstances behind Mr. Giuliani’s repeated contacts with a reporter, John Solomon of The Hill, and marshals a case that Mr. Solomon’s reporting helped push a false and damaging narrative about the then-serving American ambassador to Ukraine, Marie Yovanovitch.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/16/opinion/16ambinder/merlin_164446077_08162479-7baa-4461-b11e-50e3f5714c9a-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/16/opinion/16ambinder/merlin_164446077_08162479-7baa-4461-b11e-50e3f5714c9a-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, 