ons', 'value': 'Torres, Julio (Comedian)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Young-White, Jaboukie', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-12-17T10:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jason Zinoman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jason', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinoman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/859e5e0a-a717-5869-8803-0dff97e66e7d', 'word_count': 1062, 'uri': 'nyt://article/859e5e0a-a717-5869-8803-0dff97e66e7d'}, {'abstract': 'For Stop #47, the 52 Places Traveler reveled in nature unleashed on the new Paparoa Track, where alpine forests and craggy peaks give way to dense rainforest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/17/travel/52-places-new-zealand-paparoa-track.html', 'snippet': 'For Stop #47, the 52 Places Traveler reveled in nature unleashed on the new Paparoa Track, where alpine forests and craggy peaks give way to dense rainforest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is a bird in New Zealand that sounds exactly like R2D2. Among the competing chirps and trills of the rainforest, the bleeps and bloops of the tui, a small dark bird with the white collar of a priest, interrupted with perfect comedic timing. I found it impossible not to smile every time I heard it — and over the course of a week in New Zealand’s West Coast region, I heard it often. Soon, it started feeling like a familiar friend. On long solo hikes, soaked through with rain, calves burning, I heard the tui, beckoning me around the next bend where some new sight would undoubtedly leave me breathless.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/17/travel/17ptg-newzeala