 Russia investigation has turned out to be a dark Coen brothers farce.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/17/opinion/trump-coen-brothers.html', 'snippet': 'The great drama of the Russia investigation has turned out to be a dark Coen brothers farce.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An essential motion picture for the Trump era is one that I didn’t particularly care for when it first came out. I speak, of course, of “Burn After Reading,” the Coen brothers’ pitch-black comedy about morons attempting spycraft in Washington, D.C., which upon first viewing seemed too unremittingly misanthropic, too grimly contemptuous of its characters, without the flashes of grace that illuminate the darkness in most Coen depictions of human folly.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/17/opinion/17douthatWeb/17douthatWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/17/opinion/17douthatWeb/17douthatWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 402}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/17/opinion/17douthatWeb/17douthatWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/17/opinion/17douthatWeb/17douthatWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1371, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/17/opinion/17douthatWeb/17douthatWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/12/17/opinion/17douthatWeb/17douthatWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/17/opinion/17douthatWeb/17douthatWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Laughing Through the Trump Era', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Russian Interference in 2016 US Elections and Ties to Trump Associates', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inspectors General', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Bureau of Investigation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mueller, Robert S III', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Comey, James B', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-12-17T11:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f920ada-c3f1-5576-b6d6-cc45fc12e0f0', 'word_count': 1325, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f920ada-c3f1-5576-b6d6-cc45fc12e0f0'}, {'abstract': 'The last election weakened our democracy. Why aren’t we preparing for the next one?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/17/opinion/disinformation-election-2020.html', 'snippet': 'The last election weakened our democracy. Why aren’t we preparing for the next one?', 'lead_paragraph': 'American democracy has a target on its back: Disinformation has become a firm fixture of political campaigns in the digital age. Given the country’s polarized politics and history of close elections, the prospect that sophisticated influence operations, foreign and domestic, could shape the outcome of the 2020 presidential race is very real — and despite the searing experience of 2016, America is not ready.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '33', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/17/opinion/17nossell-Sub2/merlin_153691782_e25016a2-581c-4406-b699-6f37976064a9-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/17/opinion/17nossell-Sub2/merlin_153691782_e25016a2-581c-4406-b699-6f37976064a9-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/17/opinion/17nossell-Sub2/merlin_153691782_e25016a2-581c-4406-b699-6f37976064a9-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/17/opinion/17nossell-Sub2/merlin_153691782_e25016a2-581c-4406-b699-6f37976064a9-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/17/opinion/17nossell-Sub2/17nossell-Sub2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/12/17/opinion/17nossell-Sub2/17nossell-Sub2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/17/opinion/17nossell-Sub2/17nossell-Sub2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fool Us Once, Shame on You. Fool Us in 2020, Shame on Us.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ill Prepared For the Next  Election', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2020', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rumors and Misinformation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Russian Interference in 2016 US Elections and Ties to Trump Associates', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-12-17T11:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Suzanne Nossel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Suzanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nossel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/52ed7a8a-bfec-5a29-b50d-6901338a81f8', 'word_count': 911, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52ed7a8a-bfec-5a29-b50d-6901338a81f8'}, {'abstract': 'Key fobs and other smart-access technologies make it easier for us to get in and out of our homes — but they’re also a privacy hazard.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/17/opinion/smart-access-tenants-rights.html', 'snippet': 'Key fobs and other smart-access technologies make it easier for us to get in and out of our homes — but they’re also a privacy hazard.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the last two decades, keyless “smart access” technologies that use fobs, pin codes, smartphone apps — and most alarmingly, biometric recognition technology — have become widely adopted, transforming the way tenants access their homes and live their lives. For those whose buildings have gone keyless, many, if not most, would be surprised to find that every time they tap into their apartment or building with a fob, a piece of reference data is created and tracked by their landlord or property manager.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/17/opinion/17McKee/17McKee-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/17/opinion/17McKee/17McKee-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/17/opinion/17McKee/17McKee-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/17/opinion/17McKee/17McKee-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/17/opinion/17McKee/17McKee-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/12/17/opinion/17McKee/17McKee-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/17/opinion/17McKee/17McKee-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Your Landlord Could Know That You’re Not at Home Right Now', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Landlords', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evictions', 'ran