Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sharon Otterman and Matthew Haag', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sharon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Otterman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haag', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/88184918-1d94-5c2a-b263-327447abaae8', 'word_count': 967, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88184918-1d94-5c2a-b263-327447abaae8'}, {'abstract': 'The 1992 feminist sensation “Women Who Run With the Wolves” has returned, as a new generation of artists embrace women’s bodies in all their hormonal, bloody glory.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/17/arts/Women-Who-Run-With-the-Wolves.html', 'snippet': 'The 1992 feminist sensation “Women Who Run With the Wolves” has returned, as a new generation of artists embrace women’s bodies in all their hormonal, bloody glory.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I first spied the book last spring, in the lap of a vaguely witchy young woman pinched between two men on the subway. Soon the book disappeared into a woven bag, her shoulder brushed against mine, and it was as if she had initiated me into a secret club. After that, I saw the book everywhere: in the hands of a tattooed solo diner while she ate a game bird in a Texas restaurant; nestled with the new releases on female rage in a Brooklyn bookstore; on Lana Del Rey’s Instagram.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/18/arts/women-wolves/women-wolves-articleLarge.gif', 'height': 429, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/18/arts/women-wolves/women-wolves-articleLarge.gif', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlarg