ualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/e96041a7-72e0-5786-9ce6-407ebfa63792', 'word_count': 1055, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e96041a7-72e0-5786-9ce6-407ebfa63792'}, {'abstract': 'More movies and shows are imagining worlds where prejudice is seemingly nonexistent and everyone is accepted.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/18/opinion/booksmart-good-boys-pop-culture.html', 'snippet': 'More movies and shows are imagining worlds where prejudice is seemingly nonexistent and everyone is accepted.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I contemplate what my ideal world might look like, I imagine one in which everyone just minds their business and does their own thing, so long as they aren’t causing any harm — directly or indirectly — to others. That doesn’t mean there isn’t conflict or that bad things never happen: Humans will still annoy one another and argue, many marriages will still end in divorce, studio execs will still make up excuses to tell the same story about Batman or Batman-adjacent characters for the hundredth time.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/18/opinion/18Harris1/merlin_165672678_a9e709e2-11cc-4c2a-8911-f5cccb0a6e35-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/18/opinion/18Harris1/merlin_165672678_a9e709e2-11cc-4c2a-8911-f5cccb0a6e35-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/18/opinion/18Harris1/merlin_165672678_a9e709e2-11cc-4c2a-8911-f5cccb0a6e35-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': Non