le': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cdbf3c10-aaee-58da-9187-bf55c6a2c6a6', 'word_count': 2569, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cdbf3c10-aaee-58da-9187-bf55c6a2c6a6'}, {'abstract': 'The male actors, many making their Broadway debuts, talk about the director Ivo van Hove’s radical approach — and offer a fresh take on holiday dressing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/18/t-magazine/west-side-story-broadway.html', 'snippet': 'The male actors, many making their Broadway debuts, talk about the director Ivo van Hove’s radical approach — and offer a fresh take on holiday dressing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Murderous knife fights, attempted gang rape, malicious slurs: These were not common motifs of musical theater when “West Side Story” first premiered on Broadway in 1957. That production — a retelling of Shakespeare’s “Romeo and Juliet” — was praised by the theater critic Brooks Atkinson in The New York Times as a “profoundly moving show that is as ugly as the city jungles.” Its story, set over the course of 48 hours on the streets of 1950s Manhattan, pits the Jets (a gang of white teenagers) against the Sharks (a gang of Puerto Rican teenagers), their abiding contempt for one another born from the country’s deep-seated racism; when Tony (a Jet) falls for Maria (a Shark), bloodshed ensues. “West Side Story” is, at its core, a tale of forbidden love and insatiable enmity — and starting this month, it returns to Broadway for the sixth time, in a revival directed by the Belgian theater provocateur Ivo van Hove.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/17/t-magazine/17tmag-westsidestory-slide-AL1P/17tmag-westsidestory-slide-AL1P-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', '