nyt://article/8001ac45-4abd-5e88-a359-fedb000001b8'}, {'abstract': 'Seeing a parent go through dementia brought out the worst in me, and showed me how to do better.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/19/well/mind/my-mothers-dementia-made-me-a-better-person-my-dog-agreed.html', 'snippet': 'Seeing a parent go through dementia brought out the worst in me, and showed me how to do better.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I could hear the mournful wailing from the street as I approached my house. No question, that was my 16-year-old Jack Russell terrier “singing the blues.” Once inside, I found Zoe in the kitchen staring at the light gray wall as she continued her plaintive tune.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/24/smarter-living/24well_mothersdimentia/00well_mothersdimentia-articleLarge-v6.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/24/smarter-living/24well_mothersdimentia/00well_mothersdimentia-articleLarge-v6.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/24/smarter-living/24well_mothersdimentia/00well_mothersdimentia-jumbo-v6.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/24/smarter-living/24well_mothersdimentia/00well_mothersdimentia-superJumbo-v6.jpg', 'height': 2047, 'width': 1365, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/24/smarter-living/24well_mothersdimentia/00well_mothersdimentia-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/12/24/smarter-living/24well_mothersdimentia/00well_mothersdimentia-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/24/smarter-living/24well_mothersdimentia/00well_mothersdimentia-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'My Mother’s Dementia Made Me a Better Person. My Dog Agreed.', 'kicker': 'Voices', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Watching Dementia Steal Minds', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': "Alzheimer's Disease", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dementia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Animal Behavior', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dogs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-12-19T10:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Well', 'section_name': 'Well', 'subsection_name': 'Mind', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Petrow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Petrow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f4f3888b-d122-5d74-a229-2479f66263c8', 'word_count': 1079, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f4f3888b-d122-5d74-a229-2479f66263c8'}, {'abstract': "It's a strange question poets are often asked. This one has a good answer.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/19/magazine/poem-what-is-poetry-part-2.html', 'snippet': "It's a strange question poets are often asked. This one has a good answer.", 'lead_paragraph': 'In “What is Poetry? Part 1,” Jeffrey Thomson mentions everything from badgers and hurricanes to subways and Times Square. Somehow that makes the “Part 2” poem from his new book, “Half/Life: New & Selected Poems,” even funnier. It’s the strange question poets are often asked. Are basketball players asked, “What is basketball?” I once heard the great Argentine writer Jorge Luis Borges respond to the same question: “Poetry is so essential; it’s like the color yellow. You either see it or you don’t.” He was blind when he said this. He still saw it everywhere. Selected by Naomi Shihab Nye', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/22/magazine/22mag-poem/22mag-poem-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 114, 'width': 254, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/22/magazine/22mag-poem/22mag-poem-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 114, 'width': 254, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/22/magazine/22mag-poem/22mag-poem-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/12/22/magazine/22mag-poem/22mag-poem-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Poem: What is Poetry? Part 2', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-12-19T10:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeffrey Thomson and Naomi Shihab Nye', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeffrey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thomson', 'qualifier': None, 'title