man” to “Frozen 2,” male characters are working on their issues and feelings in stories about masculinity. But their solutions often leave out women.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Near the end of Martin Scorsese’s “The Irishman,” Frank Sheeran, the mob heavy played by Robert De Niro, asks a priest if it’s Christmas. It is a crushingly lonely moment but Scorsese isn’t soliciting our pity. Over the course of the movie, Frank has married two women, baptized several daughters and murdered countless men.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/22/arts/22Men-In-Trouble_SamWard/22Men-In-Trouble_SamWard-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 795, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/22/arts/22Men-In-Trouble_SamWard/22Men-In-Trouble_SamWard-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 795}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/22/arts/22Men-In-Trouble_SamWard/22Men-In-Trouble_SamWard-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 773, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/22/arts/22Men-In-Trouble_SamWard/22Men-In-Trouble_SamWard-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1546, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/22/arts/22Men-In-Trouble_SamWard/22Men-In-Trouble_SamWard-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/12/22/arts/22Men-In-Trouble_SamWard/22Men-In-Trouble_SamWard-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/22/arts/22Men-In-Trouble_SamWard/22Men-In-Trouble_SamWard-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Men Are in Trouble and Hollywood Wants to Help', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Woe Is He: Men Are in Trouble', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Ad Astra (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Kramer vs. Kramer (Movie)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Marriage Story (Movie)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Once Upon a Time in Hollywood (Movie)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Irishman (Movie)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Lighthouse (Movie)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Waves (Movie)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Men and Boys', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-12-19T10:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3dd3eb92-d3b2-5a6e-b5db-6289efdac780', 'word_count': 2336, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3dd3eb92-d3b2-5a6e-b5db-6289efdac780'}, {'abstract': 'Local dance, a storied Jewish quarter, historic churches and green mango curry: A rich cultural and culinary tapestry defines this colorful port city in Kerala.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/19/travel/what-to-do-36-hours-in-kochi-india.html', 'snippet': 'Local dance, a storied Jewish quarter, historic churches and green mango curry: A rich cultural and culinary tapestry defines this colorful port city in Kerala.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kerala, as they say, is “God’s Own Country,” a nod to its lush natural beauty, beguiling backwaters and wealth of spices that have been the Holy Grail for generations of seafarers. But the catchy tourism slogan could also be a tribute to the various religious traditions that have thrived there — especially in the port city of Kochi — for thousands of years. Kerala’s unique geography — hemmed in from the rest of India by the mountains to the east, but open to the world by the Arabian Sea to the west — meant the state in southwest India was a cosmopolitan melting pot for far-flung cultures. Hindus, Christians, Jews and Muslims lived side by side and traded with the Arabs and Chinese long before the Portuguese fumbled their way over (it was Kerala that Columbus was after in 1492 when he found himself in the Bahamas instead) and established the first European settlement in India at Cochin in 1500, kicking off successive waves of colonization by the Portuguese, Dutch and British. ', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/19/travel/19hours-kochi1/merlin_165669189_92db395d-670d-4820-b39e-694bdec12f61-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/19/travel/19hours-kochi1/merlin_165669189_92db395d-670d-4820-b39e-694bdec12f61-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/19/travel/19hours-kochi1/merlin_165669189_92db395d-670d-4820-b39e-694bdec12f61-jumbo.jpg', 'hei