', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTI-SEMITISM', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-12-19T20:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Barra Grant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Barra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/4637600f-261e-581d-b51b-2f66d49121ac', 'word_count': 1136, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4637600f-261e-581d-b51b-2f66d49121ac'}, {'abstract': 'In the age of quizzes, “The Great British Baking Show” and access to experts all over the world, we don’t have to settle for the traditional televised debate format.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/19/opinion/presidential-debate-alternatives.html', 'snippet': 'In the age of quizzes, “The Great British Baking Show” and access to experts all over the world, we don’t have to settle for the traditional televised debate format.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The candidate debates have become such a fixture of presidential elections in the United States that it’s easy to imagine them as inevitable and unchangeable components of our democratic process. But as we examine our democratic traditions in this fraught campaign cycle, we should take a step back and consider whether, in this age of online quizzes, audience-voted competition shows and Reddit AMAs, we could find better ways to figure out which politicians we want to vote for.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/19/opinion/19Older2/merlin_157865358_af8444a3-cd5c-45a0-9e16-f5f2fc875fc8-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {