 Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roger', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/17493303-bf48-5a48-9c55-fa60b439be2a', 'word_count': 848, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17493303-bf48-5a48-9c55-fa60b439be2a'}, {'abstract': 'At an exhibition of Sandy Schreier’s fashion collection at the Costume Institute, it’s the pieces by little-known designers that linger in the imagination.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/20/arts/design/sandy-schreier-met-museum-review.html', 'snippet': 'At an exhibition of Sandy Schreier’s fashion collection at the Costume Institute, it’s the pieces by little-known designers that linger in the imagination.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When fashion enters the hallowed halls of an art institution, as a rule, it begins to fall victim to museumitis: the need to justify its presence in the temple of high culture by focusing almost entirely on its craft or conception. This is understandable, but the result can often seem a bit arid; clothes, after all, were made for people, and it is people that animate them. To separate the life of a garment from actual life can also sap it of emotion, and meaning.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/27/arts/27schreier-review2/merlin_165528564_65767dab-890d-4b07-bd8a-05b19f099027-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 394, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/27/arts/27schreier-review2/merlin_165528564_65767dab-890d-4b07-bd8a-05b19f099027-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 394}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/27/arts/27schreier-review2/merlin_165528564_65