', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-12-23T11:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Leahy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leahy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/c17d59f5-9562-5047-b121-a9889455895b', 'word_count': 1186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c17d59f5-9562-5047-b121-a9889455895b'}, {'abstract': 'We could have been turned away at the border. But a law enforcement officer granted my family a few days of freedom.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/23/opinion/immigrant-christmas-eve.html', 'snippet': 'We could have been turned away at the border. But a law enforcement officer granted my family a few days of freedom.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the night that we first came to the United States — Christmas Eve, 1979 — my mother, my brother and I were what President Trump would probably call illegal aliens. Like most of the immigrants at America’s borders today, we were fleeing violence and instability in our native home.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/23/opinion/23weinstein/merlin_148303392_66b0f84d-8ed4-49ee-9d48-7135ae3aa82c-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 415, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/23/opinion/23weinstein/merlin_148303392_66b0f84d-8ed4-49ee-9d48-7135ae3aa82c-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 415}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/23/opinion/23weinstein/merlin_148303392_66b0f84d-8ed4-49ee-9d48-7135ae3aa82c-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 708, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_n