43f54a85-d1cc-5eef-8f35-c671f8ba0292', 'word_count': 1796, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43f54a85-d1cc-5eef-8f35-c671f8ba0292'}, {'abstract': 'The owner of Il Buco has a new space to sell tableware and home accessories from Italy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/23/dining/il-buco-vita.html', 'snippet': 'The owner of Il Buco has a new space to sell tableware and home accessories from Italy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since 2013, Donna Lennard, the owner of Il Buco and Il Buco Alimentari & Vineria in NoHo, has been selling country-style Italian tableware and home accessories from a loft near the restaurants. Now, Il Buco Vita has a new permanent home nearby. Earth tones dominate the pottery, glass, dinnerware, pitchers and serving pieces. There are rustic cutting boards, soft linens and throws, small paintings of food and landscapes, some vintage items, cookware and twisted beeswax candles with double wicks. Most of what’s on display is also sold online.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/25/dining/23burner5/merlin_166181949_51dacb22-801f-493a-8687-3f8212602928-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/25/dining/23burner5/merlin_166181949_51dacb22-801f-493a-8687-3f8212602928-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/25/dining/23burner5/merlin_166181949_51dacb22-801f-493a-8687-3f8212602928-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/25/dining/23burner5/merlin_166181949_51dacb22-801f-493a-8687-3f8212602928-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/25/dining/23burner5/23burner5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/12/25/dining/23burner5/23burner5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/25/dining/23burner5/23burner5-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Home Goods From Italy', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Shop; Seller of Home Goods From Italy Gets New Home', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Il Buco Alimentari & Vineria (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'NoHo (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Il Buco Vita (Manhattan, NY, Retailer)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-12-23T15:53:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb1c27da-0019-5796-a438-fe429efba32a', 'word_count': 123, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb1c27da-0019-5796-a438-fe429efba32a'}, {'abstract': 'This liqueur from Westchester County, N.Y., is good in place of cassis for a Kir. Or try it in a trifle.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/23/dining/blackberry-liqueur.html', 'snippet': 'This liqueur from Westchester County, N.Y., is good in place of cassis for a Kir. Or try it in a trifle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In place of cassis for a kir, turn to a blackberry liqueur from Heimat New York to add sultry richness to a glass of white or sparkling wine. (It’s also good for trifles.) It’s the latest flavor from the year-old company in Mamaroneck, N.Y. Ute Londrigan, who makes the liqueurs, has plumbed her German grandmother’s recipe box for the formula. She obtains the fruit for this liqueur, as well as for the other flavors — Bosc pear, cranberry, white peach, raspberry and rhubarb — from upstate farmers.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/25/dining/23burner2/23burner2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 801, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/25/dining/23burner2/23burner2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 801}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/25/dining/23burner2/23burner2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 767, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/25/dining/23burner2/23burner2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1534, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/25/dining/23burner2/23burner2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/12/25/dining/23burner2/23burner2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/25/dining/23burner2/23burner2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For a Festive Sip, Turn to Blackberry Liqueur', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Drizzle; For a Festive Sip, Try Blackberry Liqueur', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ALCOHOLIC BEVERAGES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blackberries', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Heimat New York', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-12-23T15:55:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a88cd5c3-b674-563f-a5ce-b486eaab7c32', 'word_count': 143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a88cd5c3-b674-563f-a5ce-b486eaab7c32'}, {'abstract': 'Afternoon tea is now available at Augustine, Keith McNally’s restaurant in the Beekman Hotel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/23/dining/augustine-afternoon-tea.html', 'snippet': 'Afternoon tea is now available at Augustine, Keith McNally’s restaurant in the Beekman Hotel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Though the restaurateur Keith McNally has tended to look to France and its brasserie style for his greatest hits, he is now taking his cue from his native England with the introduction of a proper afternoon tea at Augustine. Offered daily from 3 to 5 p.m. at his restaurant in the Beekman, a hotel near City Hall, the tea features a multitiered stand holding little sandwiches (ham, roast beef, cucumber and smoked salmon) and pastries like scones. Linzer cookies and mini-Sacher tortes are also included thanks to Markus Glocker, the Austrian chef of Bâtard, who is running the kitchen.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/25/dining/23burner1/23burner1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 404, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/25/dining/23burner1/23burner1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 404}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/25/dining/23burner1/23burner1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 689, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/25/dining/23burner1/23burner1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1377, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/25/dining/23burner1/23burner1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/12/25/dining/23burner1/23burner1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/25/dining/23burner1/23burner1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Eloise Can Now Take Her Tea Downtown', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Share; When It’s Teatime, Head Downtown', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tea', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Augustine (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Glocker, Marcus', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McNally, Keith', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-12-23T15:58:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e647ef9d-cbbb-5201-b8fd-f5e48ea9bbfa', 'word_count': 144, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e647ef9d-cbbb-5201-b8fd-f5e48ea9bbfa'}, {'abstract': 'The smallest gifts can be the ones that linger in memory.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/23/opinion/what-happened-when-my-son-asked-santa-for-a-golden-trumpet.html', 'snippet': 'The smallest gifts can be the ones that linger in memory.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NASHVILLE — It was 1994, a week before Christmas, when my not-quite-3-year-old spied a shopping mall Santa and insisted on paying him another visit. I tried to demur. I tried to deflect. His official Santa visit had taken place weeks earlier. This trip to the mall was just a chance to escape the gloom that is Nashville in December, to wear out those busy toddler legs in a place where it wasn’t raining and cold. We planned to ride a few escalators and throw a few pennies into the fountain: “I wish for a brudder!” my child yelled with every splash of a copper coin, and all the nearby shoppers smiled. I could not smile.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/23/opinion/23renklWeb/23renklWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 369, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/23/opinion/23renklWeb/23renklWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 369}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/23/opinion/23renklWeb/23renklWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 630, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/23/opinion/23renklWeb/23renklWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1260, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/23/opinion/23renklWeb/23renklWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/12/23/opinion/23renklWeb/23renklWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/23/opinion/23renklWeb/23renklWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Happened When My Son Asked Santa for a Golden Trumpet', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christmas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping Centers and Malls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Miscarriages', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-12-23T16:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Margaret Renkl', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margaret', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Renkl', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4924f3fd-dd4d-53a7-9846-56c63b51cf54', 'word_count': 1121, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4924f3fd-dd4d-53a7-9846-56c63b51cf54'}, {'abstract': 'Forget big donations. Go for structural reforms.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/23/opinion/philanthropists-college-debt.html', 'snippet': 'Forget big donations. Go for structural reforms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In May, the billionaire Robert Smith was widely lauded when he announced he would pay the student debt of Morehouse College’s graduating class to the tune of $34 million. So were Michael Bloomberg, the former New York mayor and presidential candidate, who gave $1.8 billion to his alma mater, Johns Hopkins University, in 2018, and Steve Tisch, who earmarked $10 million for middle-class students at U.C.L.A. in October. Ken Langone, a founder of Home Depot, appeared on a TV show last year to celebrate his surprise gift of $100 million to help pay the tuition of medical students at New York University. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/27/opinion/23Quart/merlin_155112423_799a6f57-1d29-4552-885b-70bf61181373-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 537, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/27/opinion/23Quart/merlin_155112423_799a6f57-1d29-4552-885b-70bf61181373-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 537}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/27/opinion/23Quart/merlin_155112423_799a6f57-1d29-4552-885b-70bf61181373-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 917, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/27/opinion/23Quart/merlin_155112423_799a6f57-1d29-4552-885b-70bf61181373-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1834, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/27/opinion/23Quart/23Quart-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/12/27/opinion/23Quart/23Quart-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/27/opinion/23Quart/23Quart-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Billionaires Don’t Understand About College Debt', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How Philanthropists Can Ease College Debt', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Student Loans', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Morehouse College', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-12-23T16:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alissa Quart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Quart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3cf63ca-4120-5401-adf7-e67a8a55729f', 'word_count': 818, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3cf63ca-4120-5401-adf7-e67a8a55729f'}, {'abstract': 'The chef Heston Blumenthal’s latest exploration of historical appetites is a menu inspired by the final meals prepared in the doomed Roman city.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/23/t-magazine/heston-blumenthal-pompeii-dinner.html', 'snippet': 'The chef Heston Blumenthal’s latest exploration of historical appetites is a menu inspired by the final meals prepared in the doomed Roman city.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In A.D. 79, a baker in the Roman town of Pompeii placed some loaves of bread in his oven. While they were baking, Mount Vesuvius erupted, raining down layers of hot ash and stone that would both extinguish life from the town and enshrine its final moments. In excavations some two millenniums later, the loaves finally came out of the oven — carbonized, but with their shape and texture intact. And on a blustery evening last week, a recreation of those loaves graced a banquet at the Ashmolean Museum in Oxford, England, where the British chef Heston Blumenthal staged a meal inspired by the cuisine of Pompeii.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/23/t-magazine/23tmag-pompeii-slide-T34R/23tmag-pompeii-slide-T34R-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/23/t-magazine/23tmag-pompeii-slide-T34R/23tmag-pompeii-slide-T34R-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/23/t-magazine/23tmag-pompeii-slide-T34R/23tmag-pompeii-slide-T34R-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credi