k': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13d0b973-b828-59fc-b9f8-fa9e84a4c3dd', 'word_count': 432, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13d0b973-b828-59fc-b9f8-fa9e84a4c3dd'}, {'abstract': 'As the United Kingdom confronts the prospect of dissolution, old factions are bracing for the possibility of new violence.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/30/magazine/brexit-northern-ireland.html', 'snippet': 'As the United Kingdom confronts the prospect of dissolution, old factions are bracing for the possibility of new violence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Belfast, like Berlin and Sarajevo, draws many visitors not despite its history of murderous conflict but because of it. Guides there take tourists to “peace walls,” the tall barricades of corrugated metal and concrete erected during the sectarian conflict, known as the Troubles, that began in 1968 and ravaged Northern Ireland for three decades. The walls were built to divide Protestant and Catholic enclaves and to prevent people from killing one another as the spiraling cycle of attacks took hold. Today tourists from around the world visit the walls and take selfies. This type of tourism is more peculiar in Belfast than in some other cities shaped by a legacy of atrocity. You can visit the intact parts of the Berlin Wall, for instance, with the knowledge that the wall no longer serves its original purpose. In Belfast, however, the walls are still there to divide, their continued presence deemed necessary to prevent a resurgence of violence.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '39', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/01/05/magazine/05mag-brexit/05mag-brexit-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/01/05/magazine/05mag-brexit/05mag-brexit-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/01/05/magazine/05mag-brexit/05mag-brexit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/01/05/magazine/05mag-brexit/05mag-brexit-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/01/05/magazine/05mag-brexit/05mag-brexit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/01/05/magazine/05mag-brexit/05mag-brexit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/01/05/magazine/05mag-brexit/05mag-brexit-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Will Brexit Bring the Troubles Back to Northern Ireland?', 'kicker': 'Feature', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Troubles Ahead', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Northern Ireland', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Great Britain Withdrawal from EU (Brexit)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders, Attempted Murders and Homicides', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Irish Republican Army', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Irish Republican Army', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Unionist Party (Northern Ireland)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-12-30T20:43:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Angelos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Angelos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/41965b91-5333-5cd0-bc57-204c7b340e99', 'word_count': 4880, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41965b91-5333-5cd0-bc57-204c7b340e99'}, {'abstract': 'Piment d’Ville began with a hotel chef’s love of the Basque spice.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/30/dining/piment-dville.html', 'snippet': 'Piment d’Ville began with a hotel chef’s love of the Basque spice.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dried Espelette chiles from the Basque Country in France offer bold heat along with rich fruitiness. Johnny Schmitt, the chef at the Table 128 restaurant in the Boonville Hotel in Mendocino County, Calif., is a fan and was buying so much of it from France that an owner, Roger Scommegna, thought it was time to start growing it. He started doing so on a small plot, and expanded to more than five acres, with the help of Ignacio Flores, a Mexican farmer. Their Espelettes are grown from seed, hand-harvested, dried and milled to make Piment d’Ville. The spice comes in regular, smoky and spicy versions.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/01/01/dining/30burner5/30burner5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 465, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/01/01/dining/30burner5/30burner5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 465}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/01/01/dining/30burner5/30burner5-