f57e74f80', 'word_count': 1558, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c81d64ab-c7bf-591a-985c-4a9f57e74f80'}, {'abstract': 'Editors’ Note for Tuesday, Dec. 31, 2019.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/30/pageoneplus/editors-note-dec-31-2019.html', 'snippet': 'Editors’ Note for Tuesday, Dec. 31, 2019.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A Bret Stephens column on Saturday quoted statistics from a 2005 paper that advanced a genetic hypothesis for the basis of intelligence among Ashkenazi Jews. After publication, Mr. Stephens and his editors learned that one of the paper’s authors, who died in 2016, promoted racist views. Mr. Stephens was not endorsing the study or its authors’ views, but it was a mistake to cite it uncritically. The effect was to leave an impression with many readers that Mr. Stephens was arguing that Jews are genetically superior. That was not his intent. He went on instead to argue that culture and history are crucial factors in Jewish achievements and that, as he put it, “At its best, the West can honor the principle of racial, religious and ethnic pluralism not as a grudging accommodation to strangers but as an affirmation of its own diverse identity. In that sense, what makes Jews special is that they aren’t. They are representational.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Note: Dec. 31, 2019', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Corrections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2019-12-31T04:17:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Corrections', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Correction', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a6fe326-ca99-59da-bde4-b2ffb9848315', 'word_count': 457, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a6fe326-ca99-59da-bde4-b2ffb9848315'}, {'abstract': 'Quotation of the Day for Tuesday, December 31, 2019.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/30/todayspaper/quotation-of-the-day-business-got-big-tax-cut-lobbyists-made-it-bigger.html', 'snippet': 'Quotation of the Day for Tuesday, December 31, 2019.', 'lead_paragraph': '“It is largely the top 1 percent that will disproportionately benefit — the wealthiest people in the world.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Quotation of the Day: Business Got Big Tax Cut; Lobbyists Made It Bigger', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Quote of the Day', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2019-12-31T04:35:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Today’s Paper', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Quote', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a0df182-326d-52cd-8cff-c48f9f1d2204', 'word_count': 51, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a0df182-326d-52cd-8cff-c48f9f1d2204'}, {'abstract': 'Best known as the most feared and frigid outpost of the Soviet gulag, Magadan struggles to keep its residents from fleeing. The ice cream isn’t enough to keep them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/31/world/europe/russia-magadan-siberia.html', 'snippet': 'Best known as the most feared and frigid outpost of the Soviet gulag, Magadan struggles to keep its residents from fleeing. The ice cream isn’t enough to keep them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MAGADAN, Russia — Like many young people in Magadan, a frigid northern Russian city more than 3,600 miles from Moscow, Dinat Yur is fed up with living in a place where winters drag on for six months and the average annual temperature is below freezing.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/18/world/xxmagadan/xxmagadan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge',