', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-12-31T09:52:04+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/d50ffd8e-a96e-5563-be6a-b0cfcf3a97ea', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/d50ffd8e-a96e-5563-be6a-b0cfcf3a97ea'}, {'abstract': 'Approach from the front. Use gestures more than words. Don’t argue.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/31/magazine/how-to-talk-to-someone-with-alzheimers.html', 'snippet': 'Approach from the front. Use gestures more than words. Don’t argue.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I don’t think anyone’s ever won an argument with a person with Alzheimer’s,” says Ruth Drew, who worked as a counselor in a hospital’s geriatric psychiatric ward before becoming a director with the Alzheimer’s Association, where she oversees the 24-hour 1-800 help line. Drew’s grandfather was afflicted with the degenerative brain disease. He would often get up before 2 a.m., sure it was morning. No amount of showing him the clock or pointing to the darkness outside could persuade him otherwise. “Everything you know about persuasion and logic you have to put on the shelf,” she says.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/01/05/magazine/05Mag-Tip-1/05Mag-Tip-1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 366, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/01/05/magazine/05Mag-Tip-1/05Mag-Tip-1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 366}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/01/05/magazine/05Mag-Tip-1/05Mag-Tip-1-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 624, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/01/05/magazine/05Mag-Tip-1/05Mag-Tip-1-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1068, 'width': 1753, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/01/05/magazine/05Mag-Tip-1/05Mag-Tip-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/01/05/magazine/05Mag-Tip-1/05Mag-Tip-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/01/05/magazine/05Mag-Tip-1/05Mag-Tip-1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Talk to Someone With Alzheimer’s', 'kicker': 'Tip', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How to Talk to Someone With Alzheimer’s', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': "Alzheimer's Disease", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-12-31T10:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Malia Wollan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Malia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wollan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5032e9be-d4d7-513d-b906-acda6a3d0932', 'word_count': 389, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5032e9be-d4d7-513d-b906-acda6a3d0932'}, {'abstract': 'They’re poorly constructed and easily confused — but mine is the only gadget I can trust.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/31/magazine/letter-of-recommendation-dumb-robot-vacuums.html', 'snippet': 'They’re poorly constructed and easily confused — but mine is the only gadget I can trust.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My phone is a honey pot for Emirati hackers, my laptop is a 24/7 advertising consultant, and my TV remote has microphones that would’ve made the Stasi blush. But just above the tracking-induced grinding of my teeth, I can hear a pleasant sound at home: the reassuring droning and clunking sound of a robot butler, smart enough to make my floors slightly cleaner but far too stupid to do anything else. This bumbling gadget obeys me and me alone. If I press its single button, it dies, while another press resumes the slow, Sisyphean tidying. There is no power higher than me and my ability to press this button, no secret god or governor buried in the privacy policy I’ll never read, nothing inside it but my grime and its cheap Chinese circuits. It is quite literally the only gadget I trust.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/01/05/magazine/05Mag-LOR-1/05Mag-LOR-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 437, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/01/05/magazine/05Mag-LOR-1/05Mag-LOR-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 437}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/01/05/magazine/05Mag-LOR-1/05Mag-LOR-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 745, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/01/05/magazine/05Mag-LOR-1/05Mag-LOR-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1491, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url