 'byline': {'original': 'By Chris Stanford', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stanford', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/01e106ae-e439-5552-90a4-7fe448d73ce0', 'word_count': 1425, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01e106ae-e439-5552-90a4-7fe448d73ce0'}, {'abstract': 'They don’t always get along. But they are both under siege by the bureaucracy of a failing health care system.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/31/opinion/doctors-nurses-and-the-paperwork-crisis-that-could-unite-them.html', 'snippet': 'They don’t always get along. But they are both under siege by the bureaucracy of a failing health care system.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Broken, wasteful, inhuman, expensive, deadly. The problems with the American health care system, or non-system, are neither subtle nor unrecognized — especially by those of us doctors and nurses who actually provide the care. And yet we all too often feel the most helpless, seeing how much of the problem is driven by drug companies and hospital networks.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/31/opinion/31BrownBergman/31BrownBergman-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 392, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/31/opinion/31BrownBergman/31BrownBergman-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 392}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/31/opinion/31BrownBergman/31BrownBergman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 668, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/31/opinio